---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CropVideo from '@/components/tools/media/CropVideo.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="crop-video">
  <CropVideo client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Cropping is not the same as resizing</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Cropping</strong> removes the parts of the frame outside your rectangle: the subject
      keeps its size, and the picture gets smaller. <strong>Resizing</strong> keeps the whole frame
      and scales it, so nothing is lost but everything gets smaller. Crop to change the shape of a
      video or to cut something from the edge of the frame. Use <a href="/resize-video">resize video</a>
      when the file must have a lower resolution.
    </p>

    <h2>Social presets, and the dimensions behind them</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Preset</th>
            <th>Where it is used</th>
            <th>Typical export size</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>9:16</td>
            <td>TikTok, Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, Stories</td>
            <td>1080×1920</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1:1</td>
            <td>Instagram feed square, LinkedIn, older Facebook posts</td>
            <td>1080×1080</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4:5</td>
            <td>Instagram portrait, the largest shape the feed allows</td>
            <td>1080×1350</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>16:9</td>
            <td>YouTube, Vimeo, presentations, most screens</td>
            <td>1920×1080</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4:3</td>
            <td>iPad, older displays, some documentation tools</td>
            <td>1440×1080</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A preset locks the shape of the rectangle and fits the largest version of it inside your frame, so
      you only drag it to the subject. The exported file has the real dimensions of the crop: a real
      vertical video, not a horizontal one with black bars.
    </p>

    <h2>Removing black bars</h2>
    <p>
      Letterboxing becomes part of a file when a widescreen clip is exported into a 4:3 frame, or a
      vertical phone video is put in a horizontal timeline. The bars are real pixels, so the only way to
      remove them is to crop them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>Remove black bars</strong> button runs the FFmpeg <code>cropdetect</code> filter over
      the first seconds of the clip. It measures how far the near-black area goes from each edge and
      suggests a rectangle. A clip that fades in from black, or one with a dark first shot, can give a
      wrong result, so check the edges in the preview before you export.
    </p>

    <h2>Cropping without a mouse</h2>
    <p>
      You can use the whole tool from the keyboard. The four number fields set the crop in exact source
      pixels, which is also the most precise method. The rectangle can take focus: press Tab to reach
      it, then the arrow keys move it one pixel, Shift with an arrow moves it ten, and Alt with an arrow
      resizes it. The accessible name of the rectangle always gives the current position and size, so
      screen reader users get the same information as the visual overlay.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the numbers are always even</h2>
    <p>
      H.264 stores colour at half the resolution of brightness (4:2:0 chroma subsampling), so a frame
      with an odd width or height cannot be encoded. The tool rounds the crop down by a pixel and shows
      the number it will use, so you do not get an FFmpeg error.
    </p>

    <h2>What the export costs</h2>
    <p>
      Cropping changes the picture, so the video is re-encoded. Unlike trimming or re-wrapping, there
      is no lossless path. The default CRF 23 looks clean for sharing. If you will edit the clip
      further, choose the high quality preset, because each re-encode adds loss. The soundtrack is
      re-encoded to AAC 192 kbps, so the result is a clean MP4 from any source container.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no watermark and no length limit, and the video is not uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
